<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lessons for Women</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Admonitions for Women, Women's Precepts, Warnings for Women</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lessons for Women, also translated as Admonitions for Women, Women's Precepts or Warnings for Women, is a work by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-zhao">Ban Zhao</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> female intellectual who lived from 45 or 49 to 117 or 120 CE, written for her daughters. The small book, of only seven chapters, describes the status and position of women in society and sets out the four virtues a woman must keep: womanly virtue, speech, manner and merit. Each is defined by restraint, so that a woman should not distinguish herself in talent and intelligence nor outdo others in skill. As one of the Four Books for Women it had wide circulation in the late Ming and Qing dynasties, and many women had its sections memorised.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Han woman was expected to obey her father, then her husband, then her adult son; the historian Ban Zhao wrote in her <b>Lessons for Women</b> that a woman’s great virtues were respect and compliance.</blockquote><a href="/societies/han-china">Social Hierarchy of Han China</a></li></ul></section>
